<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stirling</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Stirling is a city in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> on the River Forth, at what was long the lowest crossing of the river before its estuary. Its strategic position made Stirling Castle a royal stronghold, and two decisive battles of the Wars of Scottish Independence were fought nearby, at Stirling Bridge in 1297 and Bannockburn in 1314. In Roman times the area was home to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maeatae">Maeatae</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stirling.jpg" alt="Stirling" width="320" height="178" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stirling Council from Stirling, UK · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stirling_Castle_(5455709703).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The victor of <b>Stirling</b> Bridge became an enduring symbol of Scottish independence.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-23">23 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Wallace, who had won at <b>Stirling</b> Bridge in 1297, was taken to London and executed for treason.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-5">5 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>On the second day, Scottish spearmen drove the English army into the burns and marshes near <b>Stirling</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-24">24 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Scottish forces under Andrew Moray and William Wallace destroyed an English army under the Earl of Surrey as it crossed the narrow bridge over the River Forth near <b>Stirling</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-11">11 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>They seem to have been a grouping of peoples in the Forth valley and <b>Stirling</b> area that emerged in the later 2nd century, perhaps including the earlier Venicones.</blockquote><a href="/state/maeatae">Maeatae</a></li></ul></section>
